WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.440
Hi, I'm Nei Maldaner,

2
00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.640
and this is module one of

3
00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:04.380
OSWork.

4
00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.400
When an AI answer comes out bad,

5
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:07.980
the most common reaction is to switch

6
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.020
tools or write a longer and longer

7
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.100
request.

8
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.020
In this module,

9
00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:13.320
we'll do it differently.

10
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.380
You'll learn to see AI as a

11
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:16.720
system of pieces,

12
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.360
to choose the model by the task

13
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.020
and not by its fame,

14
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.640
to judge an answer with a rubric

15
00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.080
written in advance,

16
00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.120
to find out which account each cost

17
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.500
comes from, and to delegate work with

18
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:28.670
clear limits.

19
00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:30.780
We'll follow two colleagues from a school:

20
00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.200
Denise, the academic coordinator,

21
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.640
and Lúcia, who teaches eighth grade science.

22
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.320
Each lesson has a short practice.

23
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:40.220
When I ask,

24
00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:41.840
pause the video and do it right

25
00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.440
then, with a real task of your

26
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:44.080
own.

27
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.340
When we talk about AI in this

28
00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:48.240
course, we mainly mean language models,

29
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.600
the LLMs.

30
00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.800
A model is a mechanism trained to

31
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.600
produce text from what you give it.

32
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.120
Notice that last part:

33
00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:57.620
from what you give it.

34
00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.160
It can't see your school,

35
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.823
your team, or last week's meeting.

36
00:01:02.243 --> 00:01:04.043
Whatever is missing from the request,

37
00:01:04.043 --> 00:01:05.243
it fills in with the most common

38
00:01:05.243 --> 00:01:07.123
way of answering.

39
00:01:07.323 --> 00:01:08.783
Take Denise's case.

40
00:01:08.783 --> 00:01:10.323
She asked for a plan for the

41
00:01:10.323 --> 00:01:11.523
eighth grade parents meeting,

42
00:01:11.523 --> 00:01:12.923
and nothing else.

43
00:01:12.923 --> 00:01:14.123
She got back a nice plan,

44
00:01:14.123 --> 00:01:15.023
with

45
00:01:15.023 --> 00:01:16.723
a welcome, the teaching project,

46
00:01:16.723 --> 00:01:18.323
and the test calendar.

47
00:01:18.323 --> 00:01:19.783
None of that was on the school's

48
00:01:19.783 --> 00:01:20.783
agenda.

49
00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:22.603
Then she pasted the agenda and the

50
00:01:22.603 --> 00:01:24.123
minutes from the previous meeting,

51
00:01:24.123 --> 00:01:26.063
and asked it to mark what was

52
00:01:26.063 --> 00:01:27.263
still pending.

53
00:01:27.263 --> 00:01:28.403
Same AI.

54
00:01:28.403 --> 00:01:30.423
Now every item pointed to a document

55
00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:32.003
she had in hand.

56
00:01:32.003 --> 00:01:33.983
So, if a bad answer isn't always

57
00:01:33.983 --> 00:01:35.163
the model's fault,

58
00:01:35.163 --> 00:01:36.783
where does it come from?

59
00:01:36.783 --> 00:01:38.223
This is the central idea of the

60
00:01:38.223 --> 00:01:39.083
course.

61
00:01:39.083 --> 00:01:41.163
A model produces answers.

62
00:01:41.163 --> 00:01:43.263
A system organizes how those answers become

63
00:01:43.263 --> 00:01:44.463
work.

64
00:01:45.073 --> 00:01:47.603
OSWork looks at seven pieces:

65
00:01:47.603 --> 00:01:49.803
the model, the interface where you chat,

66
00:01:49.803 --> 00:01:52.343
the files that provide evidence,

67
00:01:52.343 --> 00:01:53.843
the standing instructions,

68
00:01:53.843 --> 00:01:55.683
the tools that carry out actions,

69
00:01:55.683 --> 00:01:57.103
the memory between conversations,

70
00:01:57.103 --> 00:01:59.943
and the automations that run without you

71
00:01:59.943 --> 00:02:01.743
opening the chat.

72
00:02:02.045 --> 00:02:03.485
Think of a small workshop.

73
00:02:03.485 --> 00:02:05.525
The worker's skill matters,

74
00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:07.085
but tools, materials,

75
00:02:07.085 --> 00:02:09.565
and a quality standard also decide the

76
00:02:09.565 --> 00:02:10.065
result.

77
00:02:09.865 --> 00:02:11.785
When Denise drew the seven boxes for

78
00:02:11.785 --> 00:02:12.945
the parents meeting minutes,

79
00:02:12.945 --> 00:02:14.125
she saw she had a model and

80
00:02:14.125 --> 00:02:15.105
an interface.

81
00:02:15.105 --> 00:02:16.785
The files were missing:

82
00:02:16.785 --> 00:02:18.665
the agenda was in someone else's email.

83
00:02:18.665 --> 00:02:20.415
And one note:

84
00:02:20.415 --> 00:02:22.305
OSWork is a metaphor for organization.

85
00:02:22.305 --> 00:02:24.605
You won't be replacing your computer's operating

86
00:02:24.605 --> 00:02:25.745
system.

87
00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:28.745
Separating the pieces has a practical benefit:

88
00:02:28.745 --> 00:02:30.845
it stops the reflex of writing a

89
00:02:30.845 --> 00:02:32.725
longer and longer request.

90
00:02:32.725 --> 00:02:35.085
First you ask where the failure started.

91
00:02:35.085 --> 00:02:36.805
Then you fix only that spot.

92
00:02:36.805 --> 00:02:38.765
Lúcia's AI said it

93
00:02:38.765 --> 00:02:39.965
had saved the grades,

94
00:02:39.965 --> 00:02:41.885
but the file never showed up.

95
00:02:41.885 --> 00:02:44.605
Rewriting the request wouldn't have solved anything:

96
00:02:44.605 --> 00:02:46.345
what was missing was a tool with

97
00:02:46.345 --> 00:02:48.105
permission to save.

98
00:02:48.165 --> 00:02:49.885
And there's one piece that never leaves

99
00:02:49.885 --> 00:02:51.565
the system: you.

100
00:02:51.565 --> 00:02:52.705
The model reasons,

101
00:02:52.705 --> 00:02:53.905
the tools execute,

102
00:02:53.905 --> 00:02:54.985
and you are the one who checks.

103
00:02:54.985 --> 00:02:56.505
Now pause the video.

104
00:02:56.505 --> 00:02:58.545
Take a task you've already asked AI

105
00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:00.485
to do and draw seven boxes.

106
00:03:00.485 --> 00:03:01.685
Mark

107
00:03:02.125 --> 00:03:03.428
right or wrong for model,

108
00:03:03.428 --> 00:03:04.968
interface, and files,

109
00:03:04.968 --> 00:03:06.208
and put a question mark on the

110
00:03:06.208 --> 00:03:07.968
other four, which come in the next

111
00:03:07.968 --> 00:03:09.168
modules.

112
00:03:09.188 --> 00:03:11.368
Circle the missing piece and write next

113
00:03:11.368 --> 00:03:12.328
to it how to bring it in.

114
00:03:12.328 --> 00:03:14.328
If the model is a piece,

115
00:03:14.328 --> 00:03:16.148
what kinds of pieces are there?

116
00:03:16.148 --> 00:03:17.748
Before comparing names,

117
00:03:17.748 --> 00:03:19.008
sort by function.

118
00:03:19.008 --> 00:03:20.308
There are four.

119
00:03:20.308 --> 00:03:22.468
Text models, the LLMs,

120
00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:23.928
write, summarize, explain,

121
00:03:23.928 --> 00:03:25.568
and even code.

122
00:03:25.568 --> 00:03:28.908
Image models create or edit pictures.

123
00:03:28.928 --> 00:03:31.228
Video models create clips.

124
00:03:31.228 --> 00:03:31.748
And there are classifiers,

125
00:03:31.748 --> 00:03:32.948
which

126
00:03:33.088 --> 00:03:35.628
don't chat: they receive a set of

127
00:03:35.628 --> 00:03:37.608
options and return a choice,

128
00:03:37.608 --> 00:03:38.608
a yes or no,

129
00:03:38.608 --> 00:03:40.148
or a score.

130
00:03:40.148 --> 00:03:42.328
Lúcia listed what she does with AI

131
00:03:42.328 --> 00:03:43.428
in a month.

132
00:03:43.428 --> 00:03:45.728
Summarizing the class council minutes calls for

133
00:03:45.728 --> 00:03:46.528
text.

134
00:03:46.528 --> 00:03:48.148
The science fair cover calls for an

135
00:03:48.148 --> 00:03:49.028
image.

136
00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:51.508
A ten-second intro calls for video.

137
00:03:51.508 --> 00:03:53.168
And sorting two hundred comments from her

138
00:03:53.168 --> 00:03:54.988
class into question,

139
00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:58.968
praise, and complaint calls for classification.

140
00:03:59.408 --> 00:04:00.868
Each task goes to the kind of

141
00:04:00.868 --> 00:04:02.100
model that can do it.

142
00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:06.140
ChatGPT, Gemini, and Copilot are chats.

143
00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:08.740
Behind each one there are models,

144
00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:11.560
organized into families with their own names.

145
00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:13.440
You don't need to memorize that list.

146
00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:14.640
Names,

147
00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:18.500
versions, and availability change from one month

148
00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:19.000
to the next,

149
00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:21.100
and what shows up in a colleague's

150
00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.440
account may not show up in yours.

151
00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:25.200
That's why the choice happens when the

152
00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.640
task comes up,

153
00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.260
by checking the official page and writing

154
00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:30.340
down the date.

155
00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:31.900
Denise heard that a certain model was

156
00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:33.320
the best and wanted to use it

157
00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:34.680
for everything.

158
00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:35.820
It worked for the text of the

159
00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.500
June festival invitation.

160
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.360
When she asked for the invitation artwork,

161
00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.360
she found out that model only wrote

162
00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:42.300
text.

163
00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.200
There are also hubs,

164
00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.220
like

165
00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.600
OpenRouter for text and Kie for images

166
00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:48.300
and video.

167
00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:49.920
They give you access to many models

168
00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:50.600
in one place,

169
00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.800
with their own credit.

170
00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:54.720
The classifier deserves a closer look,

171
00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.080
because it's the one people forget.

172
00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:58.840
It receives a text and a closed

173
00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:00.440
list of options,

174
00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:02.140
and it returns a choice,

175
00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:03.096
not a paragraph.

176
00:05:03.096 --> 00:05:04.996
To sort many items into a few

177
00:05:04.996 --> 00:05:06.956
categories, that can be easier to check

178
00:05:06.956 --> 00:05:08.056
than a conversation.

179
00:05:08.056 --> 00:05:09.816
It's a hypothesis to test,

180
00:05:09.816 --> 00:05:11.096
not a guarantee.

181
00:05:11.096 --> 00:05:12.416
And you can try it today,

182
00:05:12.416 --> 00:05:13.996
right in the chat.

183
00:05:14.556 --> 00:05:17.016
Lúcia pasted a student's comment,

184
00:05:17.016 --> 00:05:19.756
I didn't understand the photosynthesis part that

185
00:05:19.756 --> 00:05:20.876
was on the test,

186
00:05:20.876 --> 00:05:22.776
along with the three categories,

187
00:05:22.776 --> 00:05:24.876
asking only for the category.

188
00:05:24.876 --> 00:05:26.176
One word came back:

189
00:05:26.176 --> 00:05:27.076
question.

190
00:05:27.076 --> 00:05:28.656
She checked ten answers by hand before

191
00:05:28.656 --> 00:05:30.116
trusting the rest.

192
00:05:30.116 --> 00:05:31.496
Your practice now:

193
00:05:31.496 --> 00:05:33.456
pause and write down the three AI

194
00:05:33.456 --> 00:05:35.836
tasks you repeat most.

195
00:05:35.956 --> 00:05:37.116
Next to each one,

196
00:05:37.116 --> 00:05:38.556
the kind of model.

197
00:05:38.556 --> 00:05:40.756
Only then, a name to test.

198
00:05:40.756 --> 00:05:42.836
Within the same model there's still a

199
00:05:42.836 --> 00:05:44.356
second control:

200
00:05:44.756 --> 00:05:46.296
reasoning effort.

201
00:05:46.296 --> 00:05:48.836
The model is the mechanism you chose.

202
00:05:48.836 --> 00:05:51.116
Effort is a setting of that mechanism:

203
00:05:51.116 --> 00:05:53.596
how much it analyzes before answering.

204
00:05:53.596 --> 00:05:55.896
Denise thought changing the level meant switching

205
00:05:55.896 --> 00:05:56.896
AIs.

206
00:05:56.896 --> 00:05:58.156
It doesn't.

207
00:05:58.156 --> 00:05:59.416
With the same model,

208
00:05:59.416 --> 00:06:00.276
you can ask for a quick answer

209
00:06:00.276 --> 00:06:02.652
or a longer analysis.

210
00:06:02.652 --> 00:06:04.892
Higher levels can take more time and

211
00:06:04.892 --> 00:06:06.232
use more tokens.

212
00:06:06.232 --> 00:06:07.972
And the names on the selector change

213
00:06:07.972 --> 00:06:09.672
from product to product:

214
00:06:09.672 --> 00:06:10.652
in the chat,

215
00:06:10.652 --> 00:06:12.872
in Work, and in Codex you'll find

216
00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:14.152
different labels.

217
00:06:14.152 --> 00:06:15.952
There's no single list of levels that

218
00:06:15.952 --> 00:06:17.232
applies everywhere.

219
00:06:17.232 --> 00:06:20.012
Some chats don't even show this control,

220
00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:22.232
depending on the product and the plan.

221
00:06:22.232 --> 00:06:23.972
Many people turn on maximum effort for

222
00:06:23.972 --> 00:06:26.172
every task, just in case.

223
00:06:26.172 --> 00:06:27.772
But no amount of effort brings back

224
00:06:27.772 --> 00:06:29.612
a document that was left out of

225
00:06:29.612 --> 00:06:30.712
the request.

226
00:06:30.712 --> 00:06:32.412
Lúcia wanted to understand why the front

227
00:06:32.412 --> 00:06:35.292
office's grade spreadsheet didn't

228
00:06:35.472 --> 00:06:36.952
match hers.

229
00:06:36.952 --> 00:06:38.312
At maximum effort,

230
00:06:38.312 --> 00:06:40.232
without pasting the spreadsheets,

231
00:06:40.232 --> 00:06:41.672
she got a list of possible causes,

232
00:06:41.672 --> 00:06:43.912
without pointing to any of them.

233
00:06:43.912 --> 00:06:45.412
At the default effort,

234
00:06:45.412 --> 00:06:47.792
with both spreadsheets pasted in,

235
00:06:47.792 --> 00:06:49.412
she got the exact

236
00:06:49.412 --> 00:06:52.012
row: one student with a different grade

237
00:06:52.012 --> 00:06:52.892
on one assessment.

238
00:06:52.892 --> 00:06:55.712
The right input solved what the control

239
00:06:55.712 --> 00:06:56.872
at maximum didn't.

240
00:06:56.872 --> 00:06:58.292
So here's the order.

241
00:06:58.292 --> 00:06:59.632
Start with the default,

242
00:06:59.632 --> 00:07:02.232
which handles most everyday tasks.

243
00:07:02.548 --> 00:07:04.008
Complete the inputs.

244
00:07:04.008 --> 00:07:05.968
Only then decide whether the problem needs

245
00:07:05.968 --> 00:07:08.208
more analysis, like tasks with many steps

246
00:07:08.208 --> 00:07:09.448
or lots of data.

247
00:07:09.448 --> 00:07:11.228
And how do you know if more

248
00:07:11.228 --> 00:07:11.988
effort helped?

249
00:07:11.988 --> 00:07:14.428
By changing just one control.

250
00:07:14.428 --> 00:07:16.288
Repeat the same request,

251
00:07:16.288 --> 00:07:17.228
with

252
00:07:17.228 --> 00:07:18.568
the same material,

253
00:07:18.568 --> 00:07:19.828
in the same model,

254
00:07:19.828 --> 00:07:21.668
and change only the effort.

255
00:07:21.668 --> 00:07:23.568
If you change two things at once,

256
00:07:23.568 --> 00:07:25.528
you can't conclude anything.

257
00:07:25.528 --> 00:07:27.308
Then write down whether there was an

258
00:07:27.308 --> 00:07:28.508
improvement you can show:

259
00:07:28.508 --> 00:07:30.008
one more fact,

260
00:07:30.008 --> 00:07:31.368
one less error,

261
00:07:31.368 --> 00:07:33.248
a correct calculation.

262
00:07:33.248 --> 00:07:34.408
It got better,

263
00:07:34.408 --> 00:07:35.828
with no evidence,

264
00:07:35.828 --> 00:07:36.788
doesn't count.

265
00:07:36.788 --> 00:07:39.688
Lúcia asked for the same annotated correction

266
00:07:39.688 --> 00:07:40.188
twice.

267
00:07:40.168 --> 00:07:41.788
At the higher level,

268
00:07:41.788 --> 00:07:43.548
a unit error showed up,

269
00:07:43.548 --> 00:07:45.468
kilometers instead of meters,

270
00:07:45.468 --> 00:07:47.568
that the default had let slip by.

271
00:07:47.568 --> 00:07:49.308
She wrote down exactly that.

272
00:07:49.308 --> 00:07:51.568
Pause and do the same:

273
00:07:51.568 --> 00:07:52.748
one real request,

274
00:07:52.748 --> 00:07:54.448
two rounds in new conversations,

275
00:07:54.448 --> 00:07:56.448
and one line saying whether it improved

276
00:07:56.448 --> 00:07:58.228
and what the evidence was.

277
00:07:58.228 --> 00:08:00.168
If both come out the same,

278
00:08:00.168 --> 00:08:01.068
that's a result too:

279
00:08:01.068 --> 00:08:02.520
the default is enough.

280
00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:04.380
To say something got better,

281
00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:05.980
you need a rubric.

282
00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:08.220
A quality rubric says,

283
00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:09.460
before the request,

284
00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:11.100
which facts must appear,

285
00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:12.480
which errors are unacceptable,

286
00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:13.440
and

287
00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:15.920
how the output will be used.

288
00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.180
The key detail is the before.

289
00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:19.140
Written in advance,

290
00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:20.660
the rubric doesn't get swayed by the

291
00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:21.600
answer.

292
00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:23.860
Written afterward, it tends to approve whatever

293
00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:25.060
arrived.

294
00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:26.320
Denise was about to ask for a

295
00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:28.760
summary of the science fair planning meeting.

296
00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:30.200
I want a good summary is a

297
00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:31.400
wish:

298
00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:34.080
any well-written text passes.

299
00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.540
So, before opening the chat,

300
00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:37.920
she wrote three lines on a piece

301
00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:38.420
of paper.

302
00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:40.120
The three people responsible,

303
00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:42.260
with no deadline that isn't in the

304
00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:43.460
minutes.

305
00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:45.180
A section for pending items.

306
00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:46.320
Every

307
00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:48.480
sentence can be found in the minutes.

308
00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:50.700
Three yes-or-no questions,

309
00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:51.800
answered in under a minute.

310
00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:55.500
To start, three criteria are enough.

311
00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:56.520
Fidelity to the data:

312
00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:58.820
no data from the material changes or

313
00:08:58.820 --> 00:08:59.800
disappears.

314
00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:01.280
Agreed-upon format:

315
00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:04.240
the length and sections you asked for.

316
00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:06.340
Verifiable conclusions:

317
00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:07.960
nothing is added that can't be found

318
00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:09.360
in the material.

319
00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:11.300
These three are the template,

320
00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:12.680
and in each task they turn into

321
00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:14.080
concrete items.

322
00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:15.340
In Denise's minutes,

323
00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:17.900
fidelity became the three people responsible,

324
00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:19.580
with no invented deadline.

325
00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:22.000
Lúcia used the same template for the

326
00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:23.420
summary of a chapter from the science

327
00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:24.460
textbook,

328
00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:26.200
and the items changed:

329
00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:28.600
only concepts that are in the chapter,

330
00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:30.080
one page of bullet points,

331
00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:32.540
and each point with its page number.

332
00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:35.120
If writing criteria feels like red tape,

333
00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:36.440
start with just one:

334
00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:39.220
no data that isn't in the material.

335
00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:41.400
The others show up after the first

336
00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:42.560
wrong answer.

337
00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:44.060
Why does this matter so much?

338
00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:45.620
Because without criteria

339
00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:47.580
you pick the prettiest answer.

340
00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:49.660
Denise got two

341
00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:51.280
versions of the summary.

342
00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:53.100
The first was smooth:

343
00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:54.960
the meeting was productive and full of

344
00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.580
energy, Lúcia is organizing the groups,

345
00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:00.413
Marcos is booking the courtyard by Friday,

346
00:10:00.413 --> 00:10:02.153
the fair looks promising.

347
00:10:02.213 --> 00:10:03.413
The second was plain:

348
00:10:03.413 --> 00:10:04.993
three decisions,

349
00:10:04.993 --> 00:10:06.413
three people responsible,

350
00:10:06.413 --> 00:10:08.433
and one pending item.

351
00:10:08.433 --> 00:10:09.353
With the rubric,

352
00:10:09.353 --> 00:10:12.153
the pretty version failed all three items.

353
00:10:12.153 --> 00:10:13.193
Renata was missing,

354
00:10:13.193 --> 00:10:14.753
by Friday wasn't in the minutes,

355
00:10:14.753 --> 00:10:15.813
there were no pending items,

356
00:10:15.813 --> 00:10:17.553
and full of energy can't be found

357
00:10:17.553 --> 00:10:18.533
anywhere.

358
00:10:18.533 --> 00:10:20.913
The plain one passed everything.

359
00:10:21.113 --> 00:10:22.933
Turn the rubric into a table:

360
00:10:22.933 --> 00:10:25.413
criterion, expected, observed,

361
00:10:25.413 --> 00:10:27.213
and passed, yes or no.

362
00:10:27.213 --> 00:10:29.213
And keep the bad answer,

363
00:10:29.213 --> 00:10:30.393
because it reminds you what you want

364
00:10:30.393 --> 00:10:31.613
to avoid.

365
00:10:31.613 --> 00:10:33.673
Pause and write your three-line rubric for

366
00:10:33.673 --> 00:10:34.933
the next summary you're going to ask

367
00:10:34.933 --> 00:10:35.833
for.

368
00:10:35.833 --> 00:10:38.133
Testing two models costs something.

369
00:10:38.133 --> 00:10:39.793
And the question is:

370
00:10:39.873 --> 00:10:42.073
which account does that cost come from?

371
00:10:42.073 --> 00:10:43.373
A test may be drawing on a

372
00:10:43.373 --> 00:10:45.293
different account than you think.

373
00:10:45.293 --> 00:10:47.233
The first path is signing in with

374
00:10:47.233 --> 00:10:49.413
your account, by logging in to the

375
00:10:49.413 --> 00:10:50.033
chat.

376
00:10:50.033 --> 00:10:52.153
Then you use that account's rights and

377
00:10:52.153 --> 00:10:54.453
limits, which come from the plan and

378
00:10:54.453 --> 00:10:55.793
the workspace.

379
00:10:56.153 --> 00:10:57.793
Lúcia uses the chat through the school's

380
00:10:57.793 --> 00:10:58.693
account.

381
00:10:58.693 --> 00:11:00.773
When she hit the daily usage limit,

382
00:11:00.773 --> 00:11:02.273
she found out the limit belonged to

383
00:11:02.273 --> 00:11:03.433
the school's plan,

384
00:11:03.433 --> 00:11:03.933
not to her.

385
00:11:03.713 --> 00:11:05.953
And those limits change.

386
00:11:05.953 --> 00:11:07.873
The source of truth is your account's

387
00:11:07.873 --> 00:11:11.173
current settings, not what someone told you.

388
00:11:11.193 --> 00:11:14.013
The second path is the API key.

389
00:11:14.013 --> 00:11:15.473
Programs use AI through an API,

390
00:11:15.473 --> 00:11:17.753
with a key billed by usage on

391
00:11:17.753 --> 00:11:18.893
the platform.

392
00:11:18.893 --> 00:11:20.813
Subscribing to the chat doesn't give you

393
00:11:20.813 --> 00:11:22.773
a free balance for any program that

394
00:11:22.773 --> 00:11:24.353
calls the API.

395
00:11:24.603 --> 00:11:25.913
They're two accounts,

396
00:11:25.913 --> 00:11:27.773
with two bills,

397
00:11:27.773 --> 00:11:28.913
even when the model has a similar

398
00:11:28.913 --> 00:11:29.633
name.

399
00:11:29.633 --> 00:11:31.853
Denise ran a reporting program with a

400
00:11:31.853 --> 00:11:34.473
school key, and the tokens used showed

401
00:11:34.473 --> 00:11:35.573
up on the API platform,

402
00:11:35.573 --> 00:11:36.973
not on the subscription.

403
00:11:36.973 --> 00:11:38.173
The

404
00:11:38.713 --> 00:11:39.913
third

405
00:11:40.413 --> 00:11:41.793
path is hubs,

406
00:11:41.793 --> 00:11:43.473
like OpenRouter and Kie,

407
00:11:43.473 --> 00:11:44.853
with their own credit.

408
00:11:44.853 --> 00:11:46.373
Lúcia made the

409
00:11:46.373 --> 00:11:47.573
reading week posters in a hub,

410
00:11:47.573 --> 00:11:49.293
and the credit ran out in the

411
00:11:49.293 --> 00:11:50.433
middle of the batch.

412
00:11:50.433 --> 00:11:52.893
The chat subscription was still active,

413
00:11:52.893 --> 00:11:53.733
but it didn't cover that.

414
00:11:53.733 --> 00:11:55.873
The fix is to top up the

415
00:11:55.873 --> 00:11:57.493
credit in the hub itself.

416
00:11:57.840 --> 00:11:59.460
Before leaving something running for a long

417
00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:00.660
time,

418
00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:03.820
check which method is active.

419
00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:05.200
In the tool's settings,

420
00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:06.500
look at the account plan or the

421
00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:07.580
key in use.

422
00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:08.380
In module three,

423
00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:10.965
in Codex, a terminal command,

424
00:12:10.965 --> 00:12:12.000
codex login status,

425
00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:13.080
will show you this.

426
00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:14.540
And write the method on the project

427
00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:15.460
sheet:

428
00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:17.740
account, key, or hub.

429
00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:20.340
The credential itself never goes on the

430
00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:22.040
sheet, into a request,

431
00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:23.400
or into a screenshot.

432
00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:25.000
Before asking for the summary of the

433
00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:26.560
year's forty sets of minutes,

434
00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:29.120
Denise wrote: school chat,

435
00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:31.180
school account, plan limit.

436
00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:32.620
No password at all.

437
00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:34.440
Pause and make your own sheet,

438
00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:36.200
with one line for each AI tool

439
00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:37.200
you use.

440
00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:38.720
If today you only use the chat

441
00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:39.840
with your own account,

442
00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:41.180
the sheet has one line,

443
00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:42.660
and that's fine.

444
00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:44.680
If you find a key pasted into

445
00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:46.100
some document,

446
00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:47.860
delete it from there and let whoever

447
00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:49.340
manages the account know.

448
00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:50.480
With the right account,

449
00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:51.780
how far should you let the AI

450
00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:53.400
go on its own?

451
00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:54.180
In a chat,

452
00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:55.300
you send a message,

453
00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:55.980
get an answer,

454
00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:57.840
and see each step before the next

455
00:12:57.840 --> 00:12:58.340
one.

456
00:12:58.080 --> 00:12:59.540
An agent is different.

457
00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:01.672
It gets a goal and keeps going

458
00:13:01.672 --> 00:13:03.032
on its own:

459
00:13:03.032 --> 00:13:04.512
it reads files,

460
00:13:04.512 --> 00:13:06.872
creates, compares, fixes.

461
00:13:06.872 --> 00:13:08.032
That saves work,

462
00:13:08.032 --> 00:13:10.492
and it also multiplies the points where

463
00:13:10.492 --> 00:13:13.352
it can make mistakes without you seeing.

464
00:13:13.352 --> 00:13:15.692
Lúcia asked an agent to organize the

465
00:13:15.692 --> 00:13:16.572
quarter's grades.

466
00:13:16.572 --> 00:13:18.832
It read three spreadsheets,

467
00:13:18.832 --> 00:13:19.972
created a new one,

468
00:13:19.972 --> 00:13:21.672
and renamed the old ones.

469
00:13:21.672 --> 00:13:24.172
She hadn't asked for that last step.

470
00:13:24.172 --> 00:13:25.672
Without a written limit,

471
00:13:25.672 --> 00:13:27.212
an agent can burn time,

472
00:13:27.212 --> 00:13:29.392
credit, or touch the wrong file.

473
00:13:29.392 --> 00:13:31.272
Autonomy, here,

474
00:13:31.392 --> 00:13:33.332
is an authorization with scope,

475
00:13:33.332 --> 00:13:34.852
not an invitation to do anything at

476
00:13:34.852 --> 00:13:35.772
all.

477
00:13:35.772 --> 00:13:38.012
An authorization has five parts:

478
00:13:38.012 --> 00:13:40.112
the goal, the allowed files,

479
00:13:40.112 --> 00:13:41.532
the authorized actions,

480
00:13:41.532 --> 00:13:42.572
the time limit,

481
00:13:42.572 --> 00:13:43.792
and the stop point.

482
00:13:43.792 --> 00:13:45.252
Denise wrote one for

483
00:13:45.252 --> 00:13:47.072
the agent that prepares the monthly attendance

484
00:13:47.072 --> 00:13:48.272
report.

485
00:13:48.852 --> 00:13:51.822
Goal: a draft of the September report.

486
00:13:51.822 --> 00:13:53.532
Files: only

487
00:13:53.532 --> 00:13:55.772
the September attendance spreadsheet.

488
00:13:55.772 --> 00:13:56.852
Actions: read

489
00:13:56.852 --> 00:13:58.912
and create the draft,

490
00:13:58.912 --> 00:14:01.412
without deleting or renaming anything.

491
00:14:01.412 --> 00:14:04.792
Time: up to ten minutes.

492
00:14:04.812 --> 00:14:07.644
Stop: deliver the draft and stop before

493
00:14:07.644 --> 00:14:08.704
sending.

494
00:14:08.704 --> 00:14:10.464
It took two minutes and fits on

495
00:14:10.464 --> 00:14:11.864
a sticky note.

496
00:14:11.864 --> 00:14:12.924
And note:

497
00:14:12.924 --> 00:14:16.764
sending, publishing, and deleting are external actions.

498
00:14:16.764 --> 00:14:19.604
Each one requires an explicit instruction.

499
00:14:19.604 --> 00:14:21.544
The fact that a send button exists

500
00:14:21.544 --> 00:14:24.604
doesn't mean the agent can use it.

501
00:14:24.784 --> 00:14:26.564
The tool limits what's possible;

502
00:14:26.564 --> 00:14:29.464
the authorization says what's wanted.

503
00:14:29.604 --> 00:14:30.884
To wrap up,

504
00:14:30.884 --> 00:14:33.244
always ask for a three-part output:

505
00:14:33.244 --> 00:14:34.284
what was done,

506
00:14:34.284 --> 00:14:35.584
how it was verified,

507
00:14:35.584 --> 00:14:36.844
and what's still pending.

508
00:14:36.844 --> 00:14:39.024
That way the result becomes something you

509
00:14:39.024 --> 00:14:41.024
review, not something you have to take

510
00:14:41.024 --> 00:14:42.364
on faith.

511
00:14:42.724 --> 00:14:45.424
Denise's agent answered like this.

512
00:14:45.424 --> 00:14:49.264
Done: the draft with attendance by class.

513
00:14:49.424 --> 00:14:50.384
Verified:

514
00:14:50.384 --> 00:14:52.364
each class total matches the sum of

515
00:14:52.364 --> 00:14:53.344
its rows.

516
00:14:53.344 --> 00:14:56.324
Pending: eighth grade class C has two

517
00:14:56.324 --> 00:14:57.664
rows with no date,

518
00:14:57.664 --> 00:14:59.384
which it didn't fill in.

519
00:14:59.384 --> 00:15:01.344
Notice that the pending item wasn't filled

520
00:15:01.344 --> 00:15:02.484
with a guess.

521
00:15:02.484 --> 00:15:03.764
Denise checked one of the numbers in

522
00:15:03.764 --> 00:15:05.444
the spreadsheet and approved the rest.

523
00:15:05.444 --> 00:15:07.124
Your practice:

524
00:15:07.584 --> 00:15:10.256
pause and write a five-part authorization for

525
00:15:10.256 --> 00:15:11.016
a small task,

526
00:15:11.016 --> 00:15:12.596
with fictional data,

527
00:15:12.596 --> 00:15:14.796
and send it in the chat.

528
00:15:14.836 --> 00:15:16.456
Read the closing and check one of

529
00:15:16.456 --> 00:15:17.676
the points yourself,

530
00:15:17.676 --> 00:15:19.576
straight against the material.

531
00:15:19.816 --> 00:15:21.156
Let's put it all together.

532
00:15:21.156 --> 00:15:22.556
When AI gets it wrong,

533
00:15:22.556 --> 00:15:24.056
you look for the missing piece before

534
00:15:24.056 --> 00:15:25.396
rewriting the request.

535
00:15:25.396 --> 00:15:26.436
You

536
00:15:26.436 --> 00:15:27.996
choose the kind of model by function,

537
00:15:27.996 --> 00:15:29.756
before the name.

538
00:15:29.756 --> 00:15:31.196
You test one control at a time

539
00:15:31.196 --> 00:15:33.916
and only accept an improvement with evidence.

540
00:15:33.916 --> 00:15:36.096
You write the rubric before asking and

541
00:15:36.096 --> 00:15:37.656
decide by the rubric,

542
00:15:37.656 --> 00:15:39.176
not by the tone.

543
00:15:39.176 --> 00:15:41.096
You know which account each cost comes

544
00:15:41.096 --> 00:15:43.376
from, and you write down the method,

545
00:15:43.376 --> 00:15:44.496
never the credential.

546
00:15:44.496 --> 00:15:46.756
And you delegate with scope,

547
00:15:46.756 --> 00:15:48.096
a stop point,

548
00:15:48.096 --> 00:15:49.056
and

549
00:15:49.056 --> 00:15:51.696
a closing that says what was done,

550
00:15:51.696 --> 00:15:53.776
verified, and pending.

551
00:15:53.776 --> 00:15:55.476
When you have about thirty minutes,

552
00:15:55.476 --> 00:15:56.276
do the module lab,

553
00:15:56.276 --> 00:15:59.176
in the supplementary material for lesson six:

554
00:15:59.176 --> 00:16:00.896
the same request in two models,

555
00:16:00.896 --> 00:16:02.156
judged by your rubric.

556
00:16:02.156 --> 00:16:03.736
In the next module,

557
00:16:03.736 --> 00:16:04.976
we'll look at Chat,

558
00:16:04.976 --> 00:16:06.656
Work, and Desktop,

559
00:16:06.876 --> 00:16:08.616
three ways to work with AI,

560
00:16:08.616 --> 00:16:09.556
and

561
00:16:09.556 --> 00:16:11.636
when to use each one.

562
00:16:11.636 --> 00:16:12.688
See you there.
